**assistant**:
> `javascript` metadata **is allowed by `Registry.register_tool`** and `ToolSpec.metadata`. The problem is that your public facade drops it:
> 
> ```python
> # planner/planner2.py
> @classmethod
> def register_tool(...):
>     ...
> ```
> 
> does not accept `metadata`, so `planner_cls.register_tool(..., metadata={...})` raises `TypeError`.
> 
> Add it to the facade and forward it:
> 
> ```python
> # planner/planner2.py
> @classmethod
> def register_tool(
>     cls,
>     *,
>     name: str,
>     description: str,
>     fn: Callable[..., Any],
>     config: ToolConfig | Mapping[str, Any] | None = None,
>     quality_prior: float | None = None,
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None,
>     version: str = "1",
>     metadata: Mapping[str, Any] | None = None,
> ) -> ToolSpec:
>     return cls._shared_registry.register_tool(
>         name=name,
>         description=description,
>         fn=fn,
>         config=config,
>         quality_prior=quality_prior,
>         cost_estimate=cost_estimate,
>         version=version,
>         metadata=metadata,
>     )
> ```
> 
> The registry already supports this:
> 
> ```python
> Registry.register_tool(..., metadata=...)
> ToolSpec(..., metadata=self._frozen_mapping(metadata))
> ```
> 
> and `javascript_tools(registry)` correctly interprets and validates only the tools which declare `metadata["javascript"]`.
> 
> So the intended architecture is sound:
> 
> - Generic registry stores extension metadata.
> - JavaScript subsystem consumes and validates the `"javascript"` extension.
> - Tools not marked with this metadata are not exposed to JS.
> 
> One related issue: `Planner.register_tool` always registers on `_shared_registry`, while `register_default_tools(..., registry, ...)` receives a potentially different registry. Either ensure the passed registry is the shared one, or register directly on `registry` in that initialization path.
