**assistant**:
> This is a **concurrent lazy-import race in the OpenAI Python SDK**, not a web-search/API failure.
> 
> `ParallelDirectWebJavaScriptResponder` starts the direct model call and web search in separate threads. On their first use, one accesses `client.chat` while the other accesses `client.responses`. Those SDK properties lazily import resource modules, which have circular import relationships. Python detects the cross-thread module-lock cycle and raises `_DeadlockError`.
> 
> The later error is expected wrapping behavior:
> 
> ```text
> _DeadlockError
> → web-search adapter returns metadata["ok"] = False / provider_error
> → _run_tools raises RuntimeError
> ```
> 
> Initialize the SDK’s lazy resources **once, serially, before any `ThreadPoolExecutor` work**.
> 
> ```python
> # e.g. planner/openai_init.py
> from __future__ import annotations
> from threading import Lock
> 
> _lock = Lock()
> _initialized = False
> 
> def initialize_openai_resources(client) -> None:
>     global _initialized
>     with _lock:
>         if _initialized:
>             return
> 
>         # Property access performs SDK-local imports; no API requests occur.
>         _ = client.chat
>         _ = client.responses
> 
>         _initialized = True
> ```
> 
> Call it while registering/creating OpenAI-backed defaults, before requests can execute concurrently:
> 
> ```python
> # default_tools.py
> from .openai_init import initialize_openai_resources
> 
> adapter = OpenAIAdapter.from_env("gpt-5.4-mini")
> initialize_openai_resources(adapter.client)
> 
> tool = openai_web_search.build_openai_web_search_tool(adapter.client)
> ```
> 
> Ideally, make this part of `OpenAIAdapter.from_env()` or its constructor, so every OpenAI endpoint is warmed consistently.
> 
> If your direct model adapter and web-search tool use **different** `OpenAI` clients, that is fine: imports are process-global, and the shared lock still serializes first initialization. Touch every endpoint family your adapters lazily use (`chat`, `responses`, perhaps `images`) inside the same lock.
> 
> Also update the `openai` package to its latest version compatible with Python 3.14. This is SDK import behavior rather than an OpenAI service issue, and newer releases may reduce or eliminate the problematic import structure.
