> means the tool successfully retrieved sources, but created **no `SourceExcerpt`s**. Your responder injects only `result.source_excerpts` into the phase-2 model context, so phase 2 presently receives none of the retrieval results.
> 
> Fix `openai_web_search` to produce bounded excerpts—e.g., title, URL, and 500–1,000-character snippets—for the best $N$ results:
> 
> ```python
> return ToolResult(
>     artifacts=artifacts,
>     source_excerpts=excerpts,  # currently missing / empty
>     metadata={"ok": True, ...},
> )
> ```
> 
> Without that, the model cannot use the search result.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. How to see whether the LLM requested JavaScript
> 
> A plan is intentionally withheld from visible output. After exhaustion, inspect:
> 
> ```python
> print(planner.explain(response.id))
> ```
> 
> but extend `explain()`:
> 
> ```python
> runs = self.repository.execution_runs_for_response(response_id)
> 
> return {
>     ...,
>     "execution_runs": [
>         {
>             "id": run.id,
>             "parent_run_id": run.parent_run_id,
>             "status": run.status,
>             "source": run.source,
>             "plan_source": run.plan_source,
>             "result": run.result,
>             "error": run.error,
>         }
>         for run in runs
>     ],
> }
> ```
> 
> If `execution_runs` is empty, the phase-2 LLM did not emit a terminal ```plan block. If populated, `plan_source` is the exact requested JavaScript.
> 
> Finally, the 34-second wait happens because `_phase_two_context()` waits for both private drafting and search **before returning the stream iterator**. Move that call inside the `chunks()` generator if you want `stream_response()` itself to return immediately; it still cannot honestly emit a final grounded answer until phase 2 begins.
